<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <application
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:label="Valo Deep Links"
        android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Material.NoActionBar">
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
                DEEP LINKS: "open MainActivity for any valo://app/... URL".

                An <intent-filter> is how Android decides which Activity can handle an
                IMPLICIT Intent (one that says what to do, not which class to start). A deep
                link is exactly that: Intent(ACTION_VIEW, Uri.parse("valo://app/career")).
                For the Intent to reach us, ALL of these must match:

                  <action VIEW>         The standard action for "show the thing at this URI".
                                        Browsers, chat apps and adb all send VIEW for links.

                  <category DEFAULT>    startActivity() silently adds CATEGORY_DEFAULT to
                                        every implicit Intent. Without this line the filter
                                        never matches, and nothing happens. Easy to forget.

                  <category BROWSABLE>  Allows web browsers (and apps that open links the way a
                                        browser does) to start us. Without it, a link tapped in
                                        Chrome is ignored even though adb still works.

                  <data scheme host>    Which URIs. Here: scheme "valo", host "app", any path.

                WHY NO android:path / pathPrefix HERE?
                The manifest only decides "this URI belongs to this app". Picking the SCREEN
                from the path (/agent/..., /career) is the NavController's job, using the
                navDeepLink patterns in ValoNavApp.kt. So adding a new deep-linkable screen
                needs no manifest change, as long as it stays under valo://app.
                A URI like valo://app/nonsense still opens the app (it passes this filter),
                but matches no navDeepLink, so you simply land on the Lobby.

                CUSTOM SCHEME vs HTTPS ("App Links")
                valo:// is a custom scheme: easy to test, no website needed. But any app can
                claim the same scheme, and browsers don't make it clickable as plain text.
                Production apps usually use https://valo.example.com/... with
                android:autoVerify="true" plus an assetlinks.json file on that domain, which
                proves you own it so Android opens your app without asking.
                The navDeepLink code would stay the same, only the base URL changes.

                The other attributes on <activity> above:
                  exported="true"        Other apps may start this Activity. Required for the
                                         launcher AND for deep links. It also means anyone can
                                         send any URL, so treat the values as untrusted.
                  launchMode="singleTop" If MainActivity is already on top of its task, Android
                                         reuses it and calls onNewIntent() instead of stacking a
                                         second copy (two copies = two separate back stacks).
                                         ValoNavApp forwards that Intent to the NavController.
            -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <data android:scheme="valo" android:host="app" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
    </application>

</manifest>
